        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Higher education rate — geographical characteristics — such as Geographical area and Density — and economic factors — such as GDP and Employment rate. Notably, the overall state of the labour market, reflected in the employment rate, influences educational participation from a gender perspective, revealing patterns of gender segregation. The Knowledge Domain node represents the domain score, calculated according to the GEI methodology. Figure 7(d) shows the BN for the Power domain. As in the previous cases, the input nodes in this instance include Geographical area, Density, GDP, and Employment rate. Another input variable in this domain is the Average number of children, capturing the dual role women often assume in both care and economic spheres. Structural learning reveals that Political power and Economic power are conditionally independent given the Employment rate, highlighting the influence of labour market participation on access to socio-political power. The Power Domain score is calculated based on its constituent variables, Political power and Economic power, in accordance with GEI guidelines.",
            "block_bbox": [
                90,
                106,
                991,
                356
            ],
            "block_id": 1,
            "block_order": 1,
            "group_id": 1,
            "block_polygon_points": [
